## STEP 3 — HYPOTHESES

[HYP] box /status unauthenticated K8s infra info-leak
class: MISCONFIG
asset: box.signageos.io/status
confidence: 95
reasoning: Unauthenticated GET returns JSON with K8s pod hostname, 64-hex process UID, Node version, and full service topology (amqp0, redis0-3, mongoDB0-3) with per-service responseTime. Zero security headers (HSTS/xfo/xcto/CSP all absent) — differential vs `/` and `/login/`. Confirmed across 30+ probes spanning 3 days with rotating pods.
evidence_needed: Fresh curl with headers + body to PoC-package the leak
verify_steps: `curl -sD /tmp/poc_box_status_h.txt --max-time 20 https://box.signageos.io/status -o /tmp/poc_box_status_b.txt`
impact: Internal infrastructure mapping (pod naming convention, service topology, process UID, Node version) enabling targeted follow-up attacks. Medium severity — reconnaissance enabler, not direct data compromise.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] box CORS static whitelist + wildcard enables cross-origin exfiltration
class: MISCONFIG
asset: box.signageos.io/ and /login/
confidence: 55
reasoning: 17 static ACAO values including `http://box.signageos.io` (plaintext HTTP) and `https://*.zdusercontent.com` (wildcard). Static whitelist — spoofed Origin NOT reflected. No `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` on any box path. The wildcard + HTTP variant expand trust boundary but without credentials flag, browser-based credential theft is blocked.
evidence_needed: Confirm no ACAO+ACAC combination under any Origin; demonstrate that the wildcard is the only theoretically exploitable combination
verify_steps: `curl -sI -H "Origin: https://evil.test" https://box.signageos.io/ 2>&1 | grep -iE 'access-control'` + `curl -sI -H "Origin: https://evil.zdusercontent.com" https://box.signageos.io/ 2>&1 | grep -iE 'access-control'`
impact: Limited — no credentials flag means no cookie/auth theft. The HTTP variant (`http://box.signageos.io`) is the most notable but requires MITM on plaintext. Low-medium severity.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] Cross-tenant security-token minting via X-Auth org UID override
class: IDOR
asset: api.signageos.io/v1/organization/{uid}/security-token
confidence: 78
reasoning: X-Auth format is `id:unsafeDecryptedToken`; org identity derived from header first-part; `{uid}` in path is client-supplied and distinct from auth-derived org. CONFIRMED MECHANISM: `/organization/{uid}`→403075, `/organization/{uid}/security-token`→403076. If authz doesn't bind path uid to auth-derived org, an org-A token could mint tokens for org-B.
evidence_needed: AUTH_HELPED — requires valid `sos login` JWT for two distinct orgs + cross-testing
verify_steps: AUTH_HELPED: 1) `sos login` mint JWT for org-A; 2) baseline `curl -H "Authorization: Bearer <jwt-A>" https://api.signageos.io/v1/organization/{orgB-uid}/security-token`; 3) observe if 200 (IDOR) vs 403 (properly gated)
impact: Cross-tenant token minting → account takeover of any org. Critical severity if confirmed.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

